>From the old D&D,  the negative material plane was a region of
anti-life. It was also the place where undead drew their power from.

In game terms:

1. Unlit region, totally dark, as plane of earth, except that corridors
are never lit.  Can't see anything of the ground at all, ever.

2. Populated by undead monsters.  Undead monsters should be
significantly stronger and hit harder than they would normally.  For
wraith purposes treat as graveyard level.

3. Magical protection of some kind is necessary for survival on this
plane -- the unaided adventurer should die instantly, since the basic
stuff of the plane is basically the same as an energy beam from a wand
of death, on all the time.

4.  Protected living creatures should suffer hit point loss every X
rounds.  If we want to be especially nasty we inflict loss from maximum
hit points every 1000*X rounds, encouraging the player to finish his
business and get out.

5. Undead creatures (including the player, if polymorphed into undead
form)   regenerate hit points as if they were wearing a ring of
regeneration every turn.

6. If possible, any living creature killed on this level rises as an
undead. Any corpse left by any creature of any vital status will
re-animate (as the slash 'em necromancer spell) after a period of X
turns.

7. No effect on creatures that are neither living nor dead (e.g.,
golems).

I imagine we could implement the positive material plane the same way,
except reversed ... living creatures regenerate while undead lose etc.
However, on positive plane protection will still be required, otherwise
undead will be instantly destroyed while living creatures will suffer
massive overload and die from the shock.  

